\details{
The function intersects each isochrone with ACS tract geometries, drops
spatial slivers (negligible overlap fragments), computes area weights,
aggregates the source ACS variables, and attaches the carrier table used
by downstream margin-of-error (MOE) propagation and rate derivation.

Results are cached. The cache key is content-aware: it folds in the
upstream isochrone identity, the actual ACS estimate values, any optional
block-group population inputs, the weight parameters, and the versions of
the spatial software stack. This prevents stale reuse when ACS estimates
change while an upstream identifier, row count, or geometry envelope stays
the same.

The function applies a five-stage policy: (1) schema validation of
\code{iso_sf} (EPSG:4326) and \code{acs_sf} (EPSG:4269); (2) defensive reprojection
to EPSG:5070 plus geometry repair; (3) per-(site, drive-time) intersection
with a spatial-index prefilter; (4) area-weight computation, where each
tract's weight is the fraction of its area falling inside the catchment,
\verb{area(catchment intersect tract) / area(tract)}, followed by a sliver
filter at \code{min_weight}; and (5) per-estimand-family aggregation that builds
the carrier tibble used by downstream MOE propagation and rate derivation.

Cache reads and writes are controlled by \code{\link[=cacs_set_cache]{cacs_set_cache()}} and the legacy
\code{options(catchmentACS.cache_intersect = FALSE)} and
\code{CACS_CACHE_INTERSECT=0} switches. Older cache entries written without the
current content fingerprint are treated as a miss and recomputed.
}
\section{Estimand families and weights}{

The primary area weight is a \emph{coverage fraction}, not a universal
normalized weight:
\deqn{coverage\_wt_j = area(catchment \cap tract_j) / area(tract_j).}
\code{spatial_total} rows use this coverage weight to allocate tract counts into
catchment totals. \code{derived_rate} rows preserve the corresponding
coverage-weighted numerator and denominator totals as carrier values, then
\code{\link[=cacs_derive_rates]{cacs_derive_rates()}} computes the ratio and its MOE.

Scalar and proxy estimands use a separate normalized mean weight:
\deqn{mean\_wt_j = area(catchment \cap tract_j) /
      \sum_k area(catchment \cap tract_k).}
\code{area_weighted_scalar_proxy} and \code{median_proxy} rows use this mean-weight
path, so their weights sum to one within a site and drive-time cell.
Legacy or defensive \code{area_weighted_rate_proxy} rows, if encountered, follow
the same normalized mean-weight proxy path; curated catchment rates are
produced as \code{derived_rate} rows from numerator and denominator carriers.
\code{population_weighted_scalar_proxy} is reserved for a future population
weighting release because \code{weight_method = "population"} currently fails
loud. \code{metadata_only} variables do not receive numeric aggregation.

Area weighting assumes, for allocation purposes, that the ACS characteristic
is uniformly distributed within each tract. The function transforms validated
inputs to EPSG:5070 (NAD83 / CONUS Albers Equal Area) before measuring areas
so numerator and denominator areas are evaluated in the same equal-area
metric.
}

\section{Carrier provenance schema}{

The returned long tibble carries a \code{"cacs_aggregation_carriers"}
attribute: an internal carrier tibble consumed by \code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}},
\code{\link[=cacs_derive_rates]{cacs_derive_rates()}}, and \code{\link[=cacs_describe]{cacs_describe()}}. It is keyed by \code{site_id},
\code{drive_time_min}, and \code{variable}, and holds the per-variable aggregation
ingredients (the weighted totals and means and their raw variances, the
weight sum, and the contributing tract count) that those downstream
functions need. The output also carries a \code{"cacs_schema_version"}
attribute and a public \code{ring_topology} column.

\strong{Degenerate-tract policy.} When \code{acs_sf} carries one or more tracts with
non-positive or non-finite area (for example a Census water tract such as
\code{01003990000} in Baldwin Co., AL that slipped past the water-tract filter
in \code{\link[=cacs_acs_prefetch]{cacs_acs_prefetch()}}), the function emits a warning for each skipped
tract and continues with the healthy subset instead of stopping. The
skipped tract \code{GEOID}s are attached as a \code{"skipped_geoids"} attribute,
which is always present (an empty character vector when nothing was
skipped). The function still stops with an error when \strong{every} tract is
degenerate, since there is then nothing to intersect; that error points
back to \code{cacs_acs_prefetch(drop_water_tracts = TRUE)}. See the Water
tracts section of \code{\link[=cacs_acs_prefetch]{cacs_acs_prefetch()}} for the primary upstream filter.
}

